Let $e \in \mathsf{GExp}$.
Now $(\mathsf{GExp}, \delta'), e \mathrel{\raisebox{0.1em}{\(\underline{\leftrightarrow}\)}} (\mathsf{GExp}, \delta), e$.

We proceed by induction on $e$.
In the base, there are two cases.
\begin{itemize}
	\item
	First, if $e = 0$, then $\mathcal{L}(e) = \emptyset$ by definition, and $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(e) = \{ \alpha \in \mathsf{At} \mid \alpha \leq 0 \} = \emptyset$, meaning that $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(e) = \emptyset = \emptyset \cdot \mathsf{At} = \mathcal{L}(e) \cdot \mathsf{At}$.

	\item
	Second, if $e = p$, then $w \in \widehat{\mathcal{L}}(e)$ if and only if $w = \alpha{}p\beta$ for some $\alpha,\beta \in \mathsf{At}$, which is true if and only if $\alpha{}p\beta \in \mathcal{L}(e) \cdot \mathsf{At}$.
\end{itemize}
For the inductive step, we first note that for $b \in \mathsf{BExp}$ and $e \in \mathsf{GExp}$, we have $\widehat{\mathcal{L}}(b) \diamond (\mathcal{L}(e) \cdot \mathsf{At}) = b\mathcal{L}(e) \cdot \mathsf{At}$.
